[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609151200-the-contract-is-one-repository"
  :adr/title "The command surface is one repository every front end pins, not a copy with a checker"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-15"
  :adr/related "adr-2609075600-the-cli-leaves-the-app"
  :adr/body "## What was asked

itonami cli を実行するとエラー。cloud-itonami-cli を確認して、また cloud-itonami-* で
共通化できる部分は共通化 repo を作って共通化して。

## What was measured

`cloud.itonami.app.commands`, `cloud.itonami.app.repo-profile` and the tables
they read existed in two checkouts (app, cli) and were reached from a third
(app/mobile via `:extra-paths [\"../src\" \"../resources\"]`). The CLI's copy
was pinned with a drift checker (adr-2609075600). On 2026-09-15 that checker
said DRIFT on three of four files: commands.edn 409 -> 414 routes, cli-aliases
+18 lines, defaults.edn rewritten. The checker worked; nobody ran it.

The 2026-09-07 split ADR wrote: \"the registry is the contract between them,
and until today that contract was implicit in a shared directory. Now it is
three files with a digest and a checker that refuses to guess.\" A contract
that each party keeps its own copy of is the shared directory again, with a
digest.

## Decision

One repository, `cloud-itonami/cloud-itonami-commands`, carrying the two
deciders and the three tables. Every consumer depends on it by git sha:
the CLI through `nbb.edn` (the file the engine reads), the app and mobile
through `deps.edn`. The app's generator writes into this repository's
checkout; the app's `commands-test` compares its routes with the table on
its classpath, i.e. at the pinned sha, so a new route fails the app's suite
until this repository is regenerated AND the app's pin advanced. That is
the ordinary dependency dance and it is visible in every step.

Namespace names are unchanged so no `require` moves.

## What this is not

Not the shared code between the ~1,400 taxonomy repositories
(`cloud-itonami-iso3166-*` marketentry x186, `cloud-itonami-lei-*`
tosmonitor x51, `schema/culture.edn` x245, measured the same day by content
hash). Those are byte-identical scaffold copies and a separate decision:
they describe themselves as standalone forkable businesses, and their
`:local/root` deps already point at kotoba-lang libraries for the parts
that were extracted before (langchain-store, marketplace). Recorded in the
root ADR of the same id; not acted on here."}]
